Final state of the account (submission accepted, report above):

**What the network computes**
- The input is a map f on {0..5}, f(p)=x[p]. Layer 0 at position p attends (nearly one-hot, argmax ≈ a function of (p, x_p) — at the last position it looks up position x₅) and copies the value found there into p's residual, so each residual states how the map iterates from its own value. Layer 1 at the last position combines those read-outs and the unembedding scores the map's **cycle structure**.

**The rule (fidelity 93.0 % vs task rule 85.2 %, S = 0.50, embed site PASSES: explained 1.000, substitute 100 %, keep 100 %, remove 36.8 % ≈ random 36.5 %, interchange 93.2 % when changed, all partial swaps ≥ 86.9 %)**
1. a 2-cycle anywhere → class 1 (98 % of such maps);
2. otherwise a fixed point with no cycle of length ≥ 3 → class 0 (95 %);
3. otherwise class 2 (fixed point + long cycle 66 %, or no fixed point 91 %) — **except** in the fixed-point-plus-long-cycle case, where the value v = x₅ at the last position decides: if f(f(v)) = f(v) and v ≠ 5, or v = 5 and the map has ≥ 2 fixed points, the map is class 0 (this last refinement lifted fidelity 92.4 → 93.0 % and is exactly the "layer 0 looks up position x₅, layer 1 scores the iterates from that value" mechanism);
4. class 3 (1.2 %) is a three-way near tie (classes 0/2/3, logit margins ~0.1); every subset with ≥ 100 members I could isolate has class-3 purity ≤ 12 %, so predicting it only loses accuracy and it is left unreproduced.

**Channels that are measured but not claimed as sites**: fp, t2 (2-cycles), long (cycles ≥ 3), v5 — they are computed by the rule but live distributed across resid0/resid1, and a linear probe of them at resid1.mid reproduces the network's substituted decision only ~76 % of the time (their R² on the last-position residual is 0.1–0.7), so claiming them there would fail interchange; only the exact token identity at `embed` is claimed, which passes every inside test.

